H('ch12-st1', 'foreign intervention in the Spanish and Chinese civil wars',
  terms=[('thematic structure', 'an essay organisation in which each paragraph addresses one theme or factor across both examples, rather than dealing with each example in turn'),
         ('comparative essay', 'an essay that identifies and explains both similarities (comparisons) and differences (contrasts) between two examples, such as two wars or two leaders'),
         ('thesis', 'the essay’s central argument, stated in the introduction and developed and supported throughout'),
         ('region (Paper 2)', 'one of the four IB regions – Africa and the Middle East, the Americas, Asia and Oceania, Europe – used to require examples from different parts of the world')],
  facts=[('How many essays must candidates write in the previous-guide Paper 2, and on what basis?', 'Two essays, each from a different world history topic', ['Three essays from one topic', 'One essay and four short answers', 'Two essays from the same topic'],
          'Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes; each essay is marked out of 15.'),
         ('What is the maximum mark for each Paper 2 essay?', '15', ['10', '20', '25'], 'Essays are marked using markbands from 0 to 15.'),
         ('When a Paper 2 question asks for examples “each chosen from a different region”, what happens if both examples are from the same region?', 'The response cannot fully meet the demands of the question and is capped in the markbands', ['It receives full marks if accurate', 'It is not marked at all', 'It is moved to Paper 3'],
          'Examiners apply the question’s requirements; ignoring them limits the mark.')],
  why=[('Explain why a thematic structure is usually better than an end-on structure in a Paper 2 comparative essay.', 'A thematic structure compares the examples directly within each paragraph, making similarities and differences explicit and keeping the analysis focused; an end-on structure tends to narrate each case separately and leaves comparison to the conclusion.'),
       ('Explain why choosing appropriate examples is important in Paper 2.', 'Examples must fit the question (e.g. two wars from different regions for a causes question); well-chosen, contrasting examples such as the Spanish Civil War and the Chinese Civil War allow detailed comparison and support analysis.')],
  contrast=[('a comparative essay', 'a narrative essay', 'A comparative essay analyses similarities and differences in relation to the question; a narrative essay tells the story of events and rarely earns marks above the middle bands.')],
  misc=[('A Paper 2 essay is best written by describing everything known about two wars.', 'Examiners reward focused analysis that answers the question; irrelevant detail and narrative limit the mark.'),
        ('Comparative essays only need to discuss similarities.', 'Compare-and-contrast questions require both similarities and differences; a response with only one cannot reach the top bands.')],
  cases=[('a plan for “Compare and contrast the causes of two civil wars, each from a different region”', 'A strong plan uses the Spanish Civil War (Europe) and the Chinese Civil War (Asia), with paragraphs on ideological, economic and political causes and the role of foreign intervention.',
          'thematic comparison in Paper 2', 'Each paragraph compares both wars on one cause, making similarities and differences explicit and supporting a judgement.'),
         ('a plan for “Evaluate the impact of the Cold War on two countries, each from a different region”', 'A strong plan selects Cuba (Americas) and Korea (Asia), considering political, economic and social impact.',
          'selecting examples to fit the demands of the question', 'Choosing contrasting countries from different regions satisfies the question’s requirements and allows evaluation.')],
  src=[('Without the German and Italian transport aircraft that ferried the Army of Africa across the Strait of Gibraltar in the summer of 1936, the military rising might well have failed in its first weeks. Foreign help did not merely sustain Franco; it rescued him at the outset.',
        'Antony Beevor, British historian, The Battle for Spain: The Spanish Civil War 1936–1939 (2006) (paraphrased)',
        'German and Italian aircraft airlifted the Army of Africa in 1936; without them the rising might have failed early; foreign help rescued Franco at the start, not just later.',
        'Beevor is a respected military historian who drew on Spanish, German and Soviet archives, giving a well-evidenced judgement on the importance of the airlift.',
        'As a narrative military history it focuses on operations; the counterfactual claim that the rising “might well have failed” cannot be proved and may underplay Nationalist strengths.'),
       ('Soviet help in Manchuria in 1945–46, above all the handover of captured Japanese weapons, gave the communists a secure base. But the outcome of the war was decided by the Guomindang’s strategic blunders and its failure to keep the confidence of the cities, not by Moscow, whose aid remained cautious.',
        'Odd Arne Westad, historian, Decisive Encounters: The Chinese Civil War, 1946–1950 (2003) (paraphrased)',
        'Soviet help in Manchuria, especially captured Japanese weapons, gave the CCP a base; GMD strategic blunders and loss of urban confidence decided the war; Soviet aid was cautious.',
        'Westad is a leading historian of the Cold War in Asia who used Chinese and Russian archives, so he can weigh foreign and domestic factors.',
        'His focus on the 1946–50 period gives less weight to longer-term factors such as the Japanese war and CCP rural mobilisation in Yan’an.')],
  cmp='Both agree that foreign help was important at an early, critical stage – the 1936 airlift in Spain and Soviet help in Manchuria in 1945–46. Source A presents foreign help as having rescued Franco and implies it was decisive, whereas Source B argues the outcome in China was decided by domestic factors (GMD blunders) and that Soviet aid was cautious.',
  ev=[('The actions of non-superpower states were more important than superpower rivalry in causing Cold War crises.',
       ['Castro in Cuba drew in the USSR', 'Kim Il-sung pressed for war in Korea', 'Mao’s China in the Taiwan Strait crises'],
       ['superpowers provided weapons and decisions (Khrushchev, Kennedy)', 'ideological rivalry framed all crises', 'Berlin crises were superpower-driven'],
       'Smaller states often initiated crises, but superpower decisions determined their escalation and resolution.'),
      ('The Chinese Civil War and the Spanish Civil War were won by the side that received most foreign support.',
       ['Franco’s German and Italian aid', 'Soviet help to the CCP in Manchuria in 1945–46'],
       ['US aid to the GMD exceeded Soviet aid to the CCP', 'CCP victory owed most to peasant mobilisation', 'Republican divisions in Spain'],
       'Foreign support was decisive in Spain but not in China, where internal factors were more important.')],
  essays=[('Compare and contrast the role of foreign intervention in two civil wars, each chosen from a different region.',
           ['Spanish Civil War: Axis aid and non-intervention', 'Chinese Civil War: US aid to the GMD and limited Soviet aid', 'effects on outcomes', 'internal factors'],
           'Foreign intervention was decisive for Franco but secondary for the CCP, whose victory owed most to internal factors.')],
)

H('ch12-st2', 'the Great Leap Forward and the famine of 1959–1961',
  terms=[('depth of knowledge', 'detailed, precise and accurate knowledge of a region, including names, dates, statistics and developments, used to support analysis'),
         ('chronology', 'the order of events; a clear grasp of chronology allows analysis of causation and change over time'),
         ('analysis', 'explaining how and why events happened and assessing the relative importance of factors, rather than describing them')],
  facts=[('How long is the previous-guide Paper 3 examination?', '2 hours 30 minutes', ['1 hour', '1 hour 30 minutes', '3 hours'], 'Candidates write three essays in 2 hours 30 minutes.'),
         ('How many essays must candidates answer in Paper 3?', 'Three', ['Two', 'Four', 'Five'], 'Each essay is marked out of 15, giving a total of 45 marks.'),
         ('What percentage of the final HL grade does Paper 3 contribute?', '35%', ['20%', '25%', '45%'], 'Paper 1 is 20%, Paper 2 25%, Paper 3 35% and the IA 20%.')],
  why=[('Explain why depth of regional knowledge is particularly important in Paper 3.', 'Paper 3 questions are on specific regional topics, so examiners expect detailed evidence (e.g. statistics on the Great Leap Forward, names of campaigns and dates) to support analysis; generalised answers cannot reach the top bands.'),
       ('Explain why candidates should avoid narrative in Paper 3 essays.', 'Narrative tells what happened without explaining why or how far; the markbands reward critical analysis, so knowledge must be used as evidence for an argument.')],
  contrast=[('Paper 2', 'Paper 3', 'Paper 2 requires two essays on world history topics, often comparative across regions; Paper 3 (HL only) requires three essays on a single region in greater depth.')],
  misc=[('In Paper 3 the more facts included, the higher the mark.', 'Knowledge is rewarded only when relevant and used to support analysis; a list of facts without argument stays in the middle bands.'),
        ('Paper 3 essays do not need a conclusion if the argument is clear.', 'A reasoned conclusion consistent with the argument is part of the top markband descriptors.')],
  cases=[('an essay on “To what extent was the Great Leap Forward a failure?”', 'A strong answer distinguishes between economic, social and political outcomes, uses figures (famine deaths, steel output), considers achievements in irrigation, and reaches a judgement.',
          'depth and analysis in Paper 3', 'The answer uses precise regional knowledge to support evaluation rather than narrating the campaign.'),
         ('an essay on “Discuss the reasons for the success of the CCP in 1949”', 'A strong answer weighs CCP strengths (land reform, PLA) against GMD weaknesses (inflation, corruption) and the impact of the war with Japan.',
          'weighing factors in a Paper 3 essay', 'Weighing factors and linking them shows analysis and allows a supported judgement.')],
  src=[('The Great Leap Forward was not simply a failed economic policy. It was a catastrophe in which coercion, terror and systematic violence were the foundation of the system, and in which perhaps 45 million people died unnecessarily.',
        'Frank Dikötter, historian, Mao’s Great Famine (2010) (paraphrased)',
        'The Great Leap was a catastrophe rather than merely a failed policy; violence and coercion were central; around 45 million died unnecessarily.',
        'Dikötter used provincial party archives that had recently become accessible, giving detailed evidence.',
        'His estimate is at the high end of scholarly figures, and some historians argue he emphasises violence over policy errors.'),
       ('The famine was caused by policy mistakes that were compounded by the political system. Local officials, fearful after the Lushan Conference, competed to report higher outputs, and the centre took the figures at face value.',
        'Yang Jisheng, Chinese journalist and historian, Tombstone (2008) (paraphrased)',
        'The famine resulted from policy mistakes worsened by the political system; officials feared punishment after Lushan; false reports were accepted by the centre.',
        'Yang used party archives and interviews; as a former Xinhua journalist and party member he had unusual access.',
        'His work focuses on the system rather than individual decisions; publication was banned in mainland China, which limits peer review there.')],
  cmp='Both agree that the Great Leap Forward caused mass death and that it was not just bad luck. Source A emphasises coercion and violence as the foundation of the system, whereas Source B emphasises policy mistakes compounded by fear and false reporting; A stresses terror, B systemic dysfunction.',
  ev=[('The Great Leap Forward was the greatest failure of Mao’s rule.',
       ['famine of 30–45 million deaths', 'industrial and agricultural collapse', 'loss of Soviet support'],
       ['the Cultural Revolution’s social and cultural destruction', 'Anti-Rightist Campaign', 'some infrastructure gains'],
       'The Great Leap was Mao’s greatest failure in human terms, though the Cultural Revolution did more long-term political damage.')],
  essays=[('To what extent did the Cultural Revolution achieve Mao’s aims?',
           ['removal of rivals (Liu Shaoqi, Deng)', 'mobilisation of youth', 'chaos and economic disruption', 'reversal after 1976'],
           'Mao achieved his short-term political aims, but the long-term ideological goals failed.')],
)

H('ch12-st3', 'debates among historians about the past',
  terms=[('historiography', 'the study of how history has been written and of how and why interpretations of the past differ and change'),
         ('school of thought', 'a group of historians sharing an approach or interpretation, such as the orthodox, revisionist and post-revisionist schools on the Cold War'),
         ('interpretation', 'an historian’s explanation or judgement about the past, based on evidence and shaped by questions, sources and context')],
  facts=[('Which historian is associated with the view that Hitler’s foreign policy followed a long-term “stage-by-stage plan” (Stufenplan)?', 'Andreas Hillgruber', ['A. J. P. Taylor', 'Hans Mommsen', 'Tim Mason'], 'Hillgruber argued Hitler had a programme moving from European to world power.'),
         ('Which historian argued that the Nazi regime’s economic crisis in 1939 pushed it towards war, a “flight into war”?', 'Tim Mason', ['Ian Kershaw', 'Hugh Trevor-Roper', 'Richard Overy'], 'Mason’s argument was challenged by Overy.'),
         ('Which historian’s concept of “working towards the Führer” explains how Nazi officials anticipated Hitler’s wishes?', 'Ian Kershaw', ['Eberhard Jäckel', 'A. J. P. Taylor', 'Alan Bullock'], 'Kershaw combined intentionalist and structuralist approaches.')],
  why=[('Explain why using historians’ interpretations can strengthen an essay.', 'Interpretations show awareness of different perspectives and that history is debated; evaluating them (their evidence and context) supports the candidate’s own judgement – a top-band descriptor.'),
       ('Explain why simply listing historians’ views can weaken an essay.', 'Name-dropping without explanation or evaluation does not show analysis; the essay should integrate interpretations into its argument and judge them against evidence.')],
  contrast=[('using historians as evidence', 'evaluating historians’ interpretations', 'Using historians as evidence cites their conclusions to support a point; evaluating interpretations assesses why historians differ and how convincing their arguments are.')],
  misc=[('An essay must include historiography to reach the top markband.', 'The markbands reward evaluation of different perspectives, which can come from historians but also from contemporary viewpoints; historiography is not compulsory.'),
        ('A later interpretation is always more accurate than an earlier one.', 'Later historians may have more evidence, but interpretations reflect their questions and context; a later view is not automatically correct.')],
  cases=[('the Taylor–Trevor-Roper debate (1961)', 'Hugh Trevor-Roper attacked A. J. P. Taylor’s Origins of the Second World War for ignoring Hitler’s ideological aims, sparking a famous public debate.',
          'how historiographical debate develops', 'The debate shows how interpretations are challenged by competing readings of the same evidence.'),
         ('debates on Partition (Jalal and others)', 'Ayesha Jalal’s The Sole Spokesman (1985) challenged the view that Jinnah always sought a separate Pakistan.',
          'revisionist interpretations in regional history', 'New interpretations can reshape understanding of key figures and encourage evaluation of earlier views.')],
  src=[('It is the function of the historian to interpret the past, and therefore historians will always disagree. Each generation rewrites history in the light of its own concerns.',
        'Summary of a common historiographical argument, based on Pieter Geyl, Napoleon: For and Against (1949) (paraphrased)',
        'Historians interpret the past and will always disagree; each generation rewrites history according to its concerns.',
        'Geyl’s work is a classic example of historiographical study, showing how interpretations of one figure changed over time.',
        'It is a general statement and may underplay the role of evidence in constraining interpretation.'),
       ('Mr Taylor has written a book that, in my opinion, will do great harm, not least to his reputation as a serious historian. He has turned Hitler into a traditional statesman by ignoring the documents that reveal his intentions.',
        'Hugh Trevor-Roper, review of A. J. P. Taylor’s Origins of the Second World War, Encounter, July 1961 (paraphrased)',
        'Trevor-Roper believed Taylor’s book was harmful; Taylor presented Hitler as a traditional statesman; he ignored documents revealing Hitler’s intentions.',
        'It shows a leading historian’s criticism of an influential interpretation at the time of publication.',
        'As a polemical review written in a personal rivalry, it is not a balanced assessment of Taylor’s argument.')],
  cmp='Both are concerned with disagreement among historians. Source A presents disagreement as natural and generational, whereas Source B presents a specific disagreement as the result of one historian ignoring evidence; A is general, B specific and polemical.',
  ev=[('Historians’ interpretations of the origins of the Second World War have been shaped more by the evidence available than by the times in which they wrote.',
       ['captured German documents and Nuremberg evidence shaped early views', 'Soviet archives after 1991', 'Hossbach debate turned on documents'],
       ['Taylor reacting against the “guilty men” orthodoxy', 'Cold War context of early works', 'appeasement debates linked to contemporary policy (Suez, Iraq)'],
       'Evidence and context both shape interpretation; their influence varies by historian and period.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the contribution of historiographical debate to our understanding of the causes of the Cold War.',
           ['orthodox, revisionist and post-revisionist schools', 'impact of new archives', 'non-superpower perspectives (Westad)', 'limits of debate focused on blame'],
           'Historiographical debate has enriched understanding by broadening the questions and evidence considered.')],
)

H('ch12-st4', 'the end of the Cold War',
  terms=[('markband', 'a level of performance in the IB criteria with descriptors, used to place an essay before awarding a mark within the band'),
         ('essay plan', 'a brief outline produced before writing, setting out the thesis, paragraph points and evidence'),
         ('best fit', 'the principle by which examiners place an essay in the markband whose descriptors it matches most closely overall')],
  facts=[('Roughly how long should candidates spend on each essay in Paper 2?', 'About 45 minutes', ['About 20 minutes', 'About 30 minutes', 'About 90 minutes'], 'Paper 2 lasts 1 hour 30 minutes for two essays.'),
         ('Roughly how long should candidates spend on each essay in Paper 3?', 'About 50 minutes', ['About 30 minutes', 'About 75 minutes', 'About 45 minutes'], 'Paper 3 lasts 2 hours 30 minutes for three essays.'),
         ('Which markband for Paper 2/3 essays includes “evaluation of different perspectives” and a “reasoned conclusion”?', 'The top band (13–15)', ['The 7–9 band', 'The 4–6 band', 'The 10–12 band'], 'The top band requires integrated evaluation and a consistent conclusion.')],
  why=[('Explain why planning for five minutes before writing improves essay performance.', 'Planning clarifies the thesis, selects relevant evidence, ensures balance and structure, and prevents narrative drift, all of which are rewarded in the markbands.'),
       ('Explain why self-assessment against the markbands helps students improve.', 'It shows which descriptors an essay meets (e.g. focus, knowledge, analysis, perspectives) and identifies specific targets, such as replacing narrative with analysis.')],
  contrast=[('an essay in the 7–9 markband', 'an essay in the 13–15 markband', 'A 7–9 essay understands the demands of the question and shows mostly accurate knowledge but is partly narrative; a 13–15 essay is clearly focused and well structured, uses in-depth knowledge to support critical analysis, evaluates perspectives and reaches a reasoned conclusion.')],
  misc=[('Long essays always score higher than short ones.', 'Length is not a criterion; focus, analysis and relevant knowledge determine the mark.'),
        ('The conclusion should introduce new evidence.', 'The conclusion should draw together the argument and give a judgement; new evidence belongs in the body.')],
  cases=[('a timed essay on “Examine the reasons for the outbreak of the Korean War”', 'A student spends five minutes planning causes (Kim’s ambitions, Stalin’s approval, Acheson speech, civil conflict), writes for 40 minutes and checks the conclusion.',
          'effective time management in essays', 'A clear plan allows focus on the question within the time limit and prevents narrative.'),
         ('self-assessment of a Paper 3 essay on Partition', 'The student’s essay narrated events from 1940 to 1947 with few judgements; comparing it with the markbands placed it in the 7–9 band.',
          'using markbands to improve essays', 'The student identified the need to turn narrative into analysis and to evaluate interpretations.')],
  src=[('General Secretary Gorbachev, if you seek peace, if you seek prosperity for the Soviet Union and Eastern Europe, if you seek liberalisation: come here to this gate! Mr Gorbachev, open this gate! Mr Gorbachev, tear down this wall!',
        'President Ronald Reagan, speech at the Brandenburg Gate, West Berlin, 12 June 1987 (extract)',
        'Reagan challenged Gorbachev to prove his commitment to peace and liberalisation; he linked prosperity to openness; he demanded that the Berlin Wall be opened and torn down.',
        'It shows the public stance of the US president at a key stage of the late Cold War, combining pressure with engagement with Gorbachev’s reforms.',
        'It was a set-piece speech aimed at Western and domestic audiences; it was largely ignored by Moscow at the time and does not show the private diplomacy of the INF negotiations.'),
       ('The Soviet system could have survived for years longer through repression and the use of force. That it did not was above all the result of the choices made by Gorbachev, who decided that the Soviet Union would no longer hold Eastern Europe by tanks.',
        'Archie Brown, British political scientist, The Gorbachev Factor (1996) (paraphrased)',
        'The Soviet system could have lasted longer through repression; its end was mainly due to Gorbachev’s choices; he refused to hold Eastern Europe by force.',
        'Brown is a leading specialist on Soviet politics who interviewed many Soviet officials, giving an informed view of Gorbachev’s role.',
        'Written soon after the events and focused on one leader, it may overstate individual agency and underplay economic decline and popular movements.')],
  cmp='Both place Gorbachev at the centre of change in the late 1980s. Source A puts the onus on Gorbachev to prove his reforms by opening the Wall, implying Western pressure mattered, whereas Source B argues the end of the system resulted above all from Gorbachev’s own choice not to use force; A is a political challenge, B a scholarly judgement.',
  ev=[('Detailed knowledge is more important than structure in achieving high marks in history essays.',
       ['markbands require accurate, relevant knowledge', 'Paper 3 needs regional depth', 'specific evidence supports analysis'],
       ['structure and focus are top-band requirements', 'knowledge without argument stays in middle bands', 'evaluation of perspectives needs organisation'],
       'Knowledge and structure are interdependent; high marks require knowledge deployed within a clear argument.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the reasons for the end of the Cold War.',
           ['Gorbachev’s reforms', 'Reagan’s policies', 'economic decline of the USSR', 'popular movements in Eastern Europe'],
           'The Cold War ended because Gorbachev chose not to defend a failing system against popular pressure.')],
)


