# ch13 Internal Assessment – the Historical Investigation
# =============================================================================================
H('ch13-st1', 'the Munich Agreement and appeasement',
  terms=[('Section 1 of the IA', 'identification and evaluation of sources: the student states the research question, explains the relevance of two sources and analyses their value and limitations with reference to origin, purpose and content (6 marks, about 500 words)'),
         ('research question', 'a focused, answerable question that guides the historical investigation, e.g. “To what extent was British rearmament the main reason for Chamberlain’s policy at Munich in 1938?”'),
         ('relevance of a source', 'the reason a source is useful for answering the particular research question'),
         ('primary source', 'a source produced at or close to the time under investigation, such as a diary, speech, photograph or government document')],
  facts=[('How many marks are available for Section 1 of the previous-guide Historical Investigation?', '6', ['4', '15', '25'], 'Section 1 is worth 6 of 25 marks.'),
         ('How many sources must be evaluated in detail in Section 1?', 'Two', ['One', 'Three', 'Four'], 'Two sources are chosen and analysed for value and limitations.'),
         ('What is the recommended word count for Section 1?', 'About 500 words', ['About 200 words', 'About 1,300 words', 'About 2,200 words'], 'The total limit is 2,200 words: about 500, 1,300 and 400 for Sections 1–3.')],
  why=[('Explain why the research question must be stated clearly in Section 1.', 'The examiner judges the relevance and evaluation of the sources against the question; a vague question makes it impossible to explain why the sources are valuable for this enquiry.'),
       ('Explain why Section 1 evaluation should be explicitly linked to the research question.', 'Value and limitations are relative to the question; for example, Chamberlain’s diary letters are valuable for his motives at Munich but limited for military readiness.')],
  contrast=[('the relevance of a source', 'the value of a source', 'Relevance explains why the source relates to the research question; value is the analysis of how its origin, purpose and content make it useful as evidence for the question.')],
  misc=[('Section 1 should summarise both sources in detail.', 'Summary earns little; Section 1 should explain relevance and analyse value and limitations with reference to origin, purpose and content.'),
        ('Any two sources may be chosen for Section 1, regardless of the question.', 'Sources must be relevant to the research question and ideally offer different perspectives or types of evidence.')],
  cases=[('a Section 1 on “To what extent did Chamberlain’s fear of bombing shape his policy at Munich?”', 'The student evaluates Chamberlain’s letters to his sisters (1938) and R. A. C. Parker’s Chamberlain and Appeasement (1993).',
          'evaluating sources against a research question', 'The letters show private motives but may be self-justifying; Parker uses archives with hindsight; each is linked to the question.'),
         ('a Section 1 on the effectiveness of the Berlin Airlift', 'The student evaluates a US Air Force report (1949) and an oral testimony of a Berlin resident.',
          'choosing contrasting sources', 'An official report and a personal testimony provide different perspectives and types of value.')],
  src=[('The Prime Minister’s letters to his sisters reveal a man convinced that he alone could save the peace. On 3 October 1938 he wrote that “I have been the means of saving the world from war”.',
        'Robert Self, ed., The Neville Chamberlain Diary Letters, vol. 4 (2005) (paraphrased)',
        'Chamberlain believed he alone could save peace; he claimed after Munich to have saved the world from war.',
        'Chamberlain’s private letters show his personal beliefs and motives, without the constraints of public statements.',
        'They were written to family members and may reflect his wish to present himself favourably; they show only his perspective.'),
       ('By 1938 the Chiefs of Staff had warned that Britain could not prevent Germany from overrunning Czechoslovakia, and that a war at that time might lead to the bombing of London with heavy casualties.',
        'Summary of the Chiefs of Staff report “The Military Implications of German Aggression against Czechoslovakia”, March 1938 (paraphrased)',
        'The Chiefs of Staff believed Britain could not save Czechoslovakia; war in 1938 might bring heavy bombing of London.',
        'It is official military advice to the Cabinet, showing the strategic constraints on Chamberlain.',
        'It reflects the cautious assumptions of the military, which may have exaggerated German air power.')],
  cmp='Both are relevant to understanding Chamberlain’s motives at Munich. Source A emphasises his personal belief in his mission to save peace, whereas Source B stresses military constraints; A is a private personal source, B official advice.',
  ev=[('Primary sources are always more valuable than secondary sources for a historical investigation.',
       ['contemporary perspective and direct evidence', 'show motives (Chamberlain’s letters)', 'raw material for original analysis'],
       ['secondary works use many sources and hindsight', 'primary sources may be propaganda or partial', 'value depends on the research question'],
       'Neither type is inherently more valuable; value depends on the question and the critical use made of the source.')],
  essays=[('To what extent was British military weakness the main reason for the policy of appeasement in 1938?',
           ['Chiefs of Staff warnings', 'fear of bombing', 'Dominions and public opinion', 'Chamberlain’s personal convictions'],
           'Military weakness was central, but Chamberlain’s convictions shaped how he responded to it.')],
)

H('ch13-st2', 'the Cuban Missile Crisis',
  terms=[('Section 2 of the IA', 'the investigation: a clearly structured, analytical argument that answers the research question using evidence from a range of sources (15 marks, about 1,300 words)'),
         ('critical analysis', 'examining evidence and arguments, explaining their significance and weighing them to reach a judgement'),
         ('perspectives (IA)', 'different viewpoints, including those of historians and of contemporaries, which should be evaluated in the investigation')],
  facts=[('How many marks are available for Section 2 of the previous-guide Historical Investigation?', '15', ['6', '4', '10'], 'Section 2 is the largest section and is worth 15 of 25 marks.'),
         ('What is the total word limit for the previous-guide Historical Investigation?', '2,200 words', ['1,500 words', '2,000 words', '4,000 words'], 'The bibliography and references are not included in the word count.'),
         ('What percentage of the final grade is the IA worth for HL students?', '20%', ['25%', '30%', '35%'], 'At SL the IA is worth 25%.')],
  why=[('Explain why Section 2 should be organised analytically rather than chronologically.', 'An analytical structure, organised around arguments that answer the research question, shows critical analysis and allows evaluation; a chronological account tends to become narrative and is rewarded less.'),
       ('Explain why a range of sources should be used in Section 2.', 'A range of sources (primary and secondary, from different perspectives) supports a balanced argument, allows evaluation of perspectives and shows research skill.')],
  contrast=[('Section 1 of the IA', 'Section 2 of the IA', 'Section 1 identifies and evaluates two sources in depth; Section 2 is the investigation, an argued response to the research question using a range of sources.')],
  misc=[('Section 2 should repeat the evaluation of sources from Section 1.', 'Section 2 should use sources as evidence for an argument; evaluation of sources may appear, but the focus is on answering the question.'),
        ('A historical investigation can be on events of the last few years.', 'The investigation must be historical; the guide states it should not focus on events of the last ten years.')],
  cases=[('an investigation on “To what extent was the removal of the Jupiter missiles the key to resolving the Cuban Missile Crisis?”', 'The student uses ExComm transcripts, Khrushchev’s memoirs and historians such as Fursenko and Naftali.',
          'structuring an analytical argument', 'The student weighs the Jupiter deal against the non-invasion pledge and both leaders’ fear of war, reaching a supported judgement.'),
         ('an investigation using Sergo Mikoyan’s account of the crisis', 'Mikoyan’s The Soviet Cuban Missile Crisis (2012) gives a perspective from inside Soviet–Cuban relations.',
          'using different perspectives', 'Including Soviet and Cuban perspectives balances US-centred accounts.')],
  src=[('I must say that it was a gamble, but it was a gamble that we believed was justified. The Americans had surrounded our country with military bases; now they would learn what it feels like to have enemy missiles pointing at you.',
        'Nikita Khrushchev, Khrushchev Remembers (1970) (paraphrased)',
        'Khrushchev admitted placing missiles in Cuba was a gamble; he justified it by US bases around the USSR; he wanted the USA to feel threatened as the USSR did.',
        'It gives Khrushchev’s own explanation of his motives, valuable for understanding Soviet decision-making.',
        'Dictated after his fall from power and smuggled to the West, it is self-justifying and may simplify motives.'),
       ('Kennedy had to balance pressure from his military advisers, who favoured air strikes and invasion, with the risk that any attack would kill Soviet personnel and escalate to nuclear war.',
        'Sheldon M. Stern, historian at the John F. Kennedy Library, The Week the World Stood Still (2005) (paraphrased)',
        'Kennedy faced military pressure for air strikes and invasion; he feared killing Soviet personnel; escalation to nuclear war was a key risk.',
        'Stern studied the ExComm tape recordings in depth, giving an evidence-based account of US decision-making.',
        'It focuses on the US side and Kennedy’s role, giving less attention to Soviet and Cuban perspectives.')],
  cmp='Both show that leaders saw the crisis as dangerous and that decisions were shaped by perceived threats. Source A explains Soviet motives in terms of encirclement by US bases, whereas Source B explains Kennedy’s caution in terms of escalation risks; A is a leader’s memoir, B a historian’s analysis.',
  ev=[('The ExComm tapes are the most valuable source for understanding the Cuban Missile Crisis.',
       ['verbatim record of US decision-making', 'reveal disagreements among advisers', 'not intended for publication'],
       ['US-only perspective', 'Soviet and Cuban archives needed for full picture', 'Kennedy knew he was recording, which may have shaped his words'],
       'The tapes are uniquely valuable for US decision-making but must be combined with Soviet and Cuban sources.')],
  essays=[('Evaluate the importance of Soviet motives in causing the Cuban Missile Crisis.',
           ['missile gap', 'defence of Cuba', 'Jupiter missiles in Turkey', 'US policy (Bay of Pigs, Mongoose)'],
           'Soviet motives were central, but US policy towards Cuba created the conditions for Khrushchev’s gamble.')],
)

H('ch13-st3', 'the methods used by historians',
  terms=[('Section 3 of the IA', 'reflection: the student reflects on what the investigation revealed about the methods used by historians and the challenges they face (4 marks, about 400 words)'),
         ('methods of the historian', 'the processes historians use, including selecting and evaluating sources, corroboration, constructing interpretations and dealing with gaps or bias in evidence'),
         ('limitations of evidence', 'gaps, bias, unreliability or inaccessibility in sources that restrict what historians can know')],
  facts=[('How many marks are available for Section 3 (reflection)?', '4', ['6', '15', '2'], 'Section 3 is worth 4 of 25 marks.'),
         ('What is the recommended word count for Section 3?', 'About 400 words', ['About 100 words', 'About 800 words', 'About 1,300 words'], 'The section should be focused and reflective.'),
         ('Which of the following is the best focus for Section 3?', 'Challenges the student faced in reconciling conflicting sources and what this shows about historians’ methods', ['A summary of the investigation’s conclusion', 'A list of sources used', 'A description of where research was done'],
          'Reflection should link the student’s experience to the methods and challenges of historians.')],
  why=[('Explain why Section 3 should be linked explicitly to the investigation.', 'Generic reflections on history earn few marks; the reflection should draw on specific challenges in the investigation, such as conflicting accounts of the Berlin Airlift, and explain what they reveal about historians’ methods.'),
       ('Explain why reflecting on conflicting sources is valuable.', 'It shows understanding that historians must judge reliability, corroborate evidence and accept that interpretations are provisional.')],
  contrast=[('reflection on methods', 'a summary of findings', 'Reflection considers how knowledge was produced and its limits; a summary restates what was found.')],
  misc=[('Section 3 should restate the conclusion of the investigation.', 'Section 3 reflects on the methods and challenges of historians, not on the findings.'),
        ('Historians can always find complete and unbiased evidence.', 'Evidence is often incomplete, partial or contradictory; historians must interpret it and acknowledge uncertainty.')],
  cases=[('reflection on conflicting death tolls for the Nanjing Massacre', 'A student investigating Nanjing found estimates from tens of thousands to over 300,000 dead.',
          'the challenges historians face with contested evidence', 'The student reflected on how nationalist memory and the destruction of records affect numbers and interpretation.'),
         ('reflection on oral testimony in an investigation of Partition', 'A student used interviews from the 1947 Partition Archive and found memories differed sharply.',
          'the value and limitations of oral history', 'The student reflected that oral history gives voice to ordinary people but is shaped by memory and the present.')],
  src=[('The historian is engaged on a continuous process of moulding his facts to his interpretation and his interpretation to his facts.',
        'E. H. Carr, What is History? (1961) (extract)',
        'Historians shape facts to interpretations and interpretations to facts; history is a continuous process of interaction.',
        'It is a famous reflection on historical method by a leading historian, useful for Section 3.',
        'It is a theoretical position contested by others, such as G. R. Elton, who stressed the objectivity of evidence.'),
       ('The historian’s primary duty is to the evidence. The past exists independently of the historian, and the historian’s job is to recover it through rigorous examination of the sources.',
        'G. R. Elton, The Practice of History (1967) (paraphrased)',
        'Historians’ first duty is to evidence; the past exists independently; it can be recovered through rigorous examination of sources.',
        'It presents an influential empiricist view of historical method from a leading historian.',
        'It was written in response to Carr and may overstate the possibility of objectivity.')],
  cmp='Both discuss the relationship between historians and evidence. Source A stresses the interaction between interpretation and facts, whereas Source B stresses the independent existence of the past and the primacy of evidence; A is relativist, B empiricist.',
  ev=[('It is impossible for historians to be objective.',
       ['selection of evidence and questions (Carr)', 'national perspectives on events such as Nanjing', 'historians’ contexts shape interpretations'],
       ['rules of evidence and corroboration (Elton)', 'peer review and debate', 'some facts are not disputed'],
       'Absolute objectivity is impossible, but rigorous method makes some interpretations more reliable than others.')],
  essays=[('Discuss the challenges facing historians studying the Cultural Revolution.',
           ['restricted archives in China', 'propaganda and destroyed records', 'oral testimony and memory', 'political sensitivity'],
           'Historians face major challenges of access and reliability, but memoirs and local archives have expanded knowledge.')],
)

H('ch13-st4', 'referencing and academic integrity in historical writing',
  terms=[('bibliography', 'a list of all sources consulted in the investigation, given in a consistent referencing style; it is not included in the word count'),
         ('academic integrity', 'honest and responsible scholarship, including acknowledging all sources and not presenting others’ work as one’s own'),
         ('consistent referencing', 'using one recognised citation style (e.g. Chicago, MLA or Harvard) throughout for footnotes or in-text references')],
  facts=[('What happens to work beyond the 2,200-word limit in the previous-guide IA?', 'Examiners are not required to read material beyond the limit', ['It is marked normally', 'It is awarded double marks', 'The whole IA receives zero'], 'Material beyond the limit may not be credited.'),
         ('Which elements are not included in the IA word count?', 'The bibliography and references (footnotes containing only citations)', ['Section 1', 'Section 3', 'The research question'], 'Only the main text of Sections 1–3 counts.'),
         ('How much written feedback may a teacher give on a draft of the IA?', 'Feedback on one draft only', ['Unlimited feedback on all drafts', 'No feedback at all', 'A full correction of each draft'], 'Teachers may comment on one draft but must not edit it.')],
  why=[('Explain why a consistent referencing style is required in the IA.', 'It allows the moderator to identify and check sources, shows academic integrity, and distinguishes the student’s ideas from those of others.'),
       ('Explain why staying within the word limit matters.', 'Examiners may stop reading beyond 2,200 words, so conclusions or reflection at the end may not be credited; the limit also tests the ability to write concisely.')],
  contrast=[('a citation', 'a bibliography entry', 'A citation acknowledges a specific source at the point it is used (footnote or in-text); the bibliography lists all sources consulted at the end.')],
  misc=[('Paraphrasing a historian’s argument does not need a reference.', 'Paraphrased ideas must be referenced; failing to do so is plagiarism.'),
        ('Footnotes can be used to include extra discussion without counting towards the word limit.', 'Footnotes containing discussion count towards the word limit; only citation footnotes are excluded.')],
  cases=[('an IA flagged for academic misconduct', 'A student copied paragraphs from an online summary of the Korean War without acknowledgement; the work was investigated by the IB.',
          'the importance of academic integrity', 'Using unacknowledged material constitutes plagiarism and can lead to no grade being awarded.'),
         ('a final check of an IA on the Emergency in India', 'The student checked the word count (2,180), the research question on the title page, consistent Chicago footnotes and a bibliography.',
          'final checks before submission', 'Final checks ensure that the work meets formal requirements and that nothing is lost through technical errors.')],
  src=[('All work submitted for assessment must be the authentic work of the candidate, and all sources must be fully and correctly acknowledged.',
        'International Baccalaureate, Academic Integrity Policy (2019) (paraphrased)',
        'Work must be the candidate’s own; all sources must be acknowledged fully and correctly.',
        'It is the official IB statement of the rules, so it is authoritative for what is required.',
        'It is a general policy and does not give specific guidance on History citation conventions.'),
       ('Footnotes are the historian’s way of showing their workings: they allow readers to check where evidence comes from and to judge how far it supports the argument.',
        'Anthony Grafton, historian, The Footnote: A Curious History (1997) (paraphrased)',
        'Footnotes show historians’ working; they let readers check evidence; they help judge whether evidence supports the argument.',
        'A historian’s study of the history and purpose of footnotes, offering insight into the role of referencing.',
        'It is concerned with scholarly practice in general, not with school assessment.')],
  cmp='Both stress the importance of acknowledging sources. Source A presents acknowledgement as a rule of authenticity, whereas Source B presents footnotes as a tool for verifying arguments; A is regulatory, B explains the scholarly purpose.',
  ev=[('Referencing is the most important feature of a successful historical investigation.',
       ['academic integrity requirement', 'allows verification of evidence', 'shows the range of sources'],
       ['the quality of analysis in Section 2 carries most marks', 'a focused research question is essential', 'reflection on methods'],
       'Referencing is essential for integrity, but the quality of analysis determines success.')],
  essays=[('To what extent do the sources available determine the conclusions historians reach about the Partition of India?',
           ['official British records vs oral testimonies', 'nationalist histories in India and Pakistan', 'new archives (1947 Partition Archive)', 'historians’ questions'],
           'Sources shape conclusions, but historians’ questions and frameworks are equally important.')],
)
